Once the event plane has been determined, one can study the particle distributions with respect to the event plane. Assuming the event plane has been determined using the \(m\)-th harmonic, the Fourier expansion can be written as:

\[\frac{d(wN)}{d(\phi-\Psi_{m})}=\frac{<wN>}{2\pi}\left(1+2\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}v_{ km}^{obs}\cos(km(\phi-\Psi_{m})\right) \tag{5.9}\]

Therefore a given coefficient \(v_{n}\) can be computed experimentally by averaging over the distributions \(\langle\cos(n(\phi-\Psi_{m}))\rangle\). It is worth noting that for a given event plane, \(m\), negative entries in the distributions can be shifted to positive ones by adding \(2\pi\)

